```jsonl
{"section": "1. System Scope and Design Philosophy", "content": "COGK is an architecture for building a multimodal reasoning system that can operate in three distinct hardware regimes: (A) CPU+RAM-only inference and data processing (baseline), (B) single-GPU VRAM-exclusive training with CPU RAM used as a data staging and classical-model execution host, and (C) fully distributed VRAM-condensed scaling where multiple GPU nodes each hold a shard of the total memory-ocean and training state, coordinated via high-speed interconnect. The immediate feasibility study targets a corpus of 2 GB of mixed-modality raw data, expanding to 10 GB by month two. This small initial volume makes VRAM-exclusive execution tractable (32 GB VRAM can hold the entire working set) while the architecture is designed to seamlessly spill to RAM when needed and to scale to petabyte-scale knowledge stores in a distributed setting without altering the fundamental learning dynamics."}

{"section": "2. Hardware Profile and Utilization Floor", "content": "The development workstation comprises: 16-core AMD Ryzen 9 5950X, 128 GB DDR5-5600 (theoretical ~90 GB/s memory bandwidth), a single NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4090 (24 GB or 32 GB VRAM, depending on variant; assume 32 GB), and a 2 TB PCIe 4.0 NVMe SSD. The CPU+RAM complex is the mandatory execution substrate for all preprocessing, embedding extraction, classical machine learning models (Random Forest, K-Means, and any future additions), simulation engines (InEM), and the final inference fallback path. The GPU is used strictly for gradient-based training of the Thinker Core transformer and, optionally, for accelerated inference when available. No component beyond the training loop and an optional retrieval hot-cache is GPU-coupled; revert to CPU is a single flag switch."}

{"section": "3. Three-Phase Execution Model", "content": "The pipeline is partitioned into three phases. Phase 1: 'Data Ingress and Indexing' — all raw data (2 GB initially) is processed by the Scalar Embedding Engine (SEE) to produce a unified 256-d vector representation. Classical models (Random Forest, K-Means) run on these vectors to establish initial clustering, outlier detection, and feature-importance maps. The resulting Multimodal Memory Ocean (MMO) index is built entirely in RAM. Phase 2: 'RAM-Resident Simulation & Curation' — the InEM simulation framework generates synthetic episodes from the base scalars, using CPU resources, expanding the training set without touching the GPU. Phase 3: 'VRAM-Condensed Training' — the compact neural core is trained on the compiled (real + synthetic) data, with all model parameters, optimizer states, and a hot-cache of frequently accessed MMO vectors residing in VRAM. The system continuously loops between Phase 2 and Phase 3, with Phase 1 repeated when new external data arrives."}

{"section": "4. Scalar Embedding Engine (SEE) Detailed Design", "content": "The SEE is a multi-process pipeline that converts raw modality blobs into normalized 256-d vectors. For each modality, a frozen, open-source pretrained model is executed using ONNX Runtime on CPU. Images: a ViT-B/32 CLIP variant outputs 512-d patch embeddings, which are then projected via a learned linear layer (weights trained offline on a 5k-image multimodal alignment set) to 256-d. Text: LaBSE (Language-agnostic BERT Sentence Embedding) outputs 768-d, projected to 256-d. Audio: Wav2Vec2-base (feature extractor only, no CTC head) yields 512-d frames, pooled over time, projected to 256-d. Binary/Code: CodeBERT-base (mean-pooled last hidden state) gives 768-d, projected to 256-d. All projection layers are L2-normalized at the output to reside on the unit hypersphere. The SEE runs with 8 worker processes, each bound to a CPU core, saturating memory bandwidth at ~85 GB/s read, producing approximately 1 million vectors per hour."}

{"section": "5. Classical Model Integration: Random Forest and K-Means", "content": "Prior to neural training, the corpus of embedded vectors (2 GB yields roughly 8 million vectors) is analyzed with two non-neural algorithms. K-Means (k = 10,000) clusters the entire vector set using the FAISS KMeans implementation on CPU, assigning each vector a cluster ID. The cluster centroids are stored in the MMO as 'concept anchors' with a special provenance tag. Random Forest (500 trees, max depth 20) is trained on a supervised task derived from modality metadata: predicting the modality type (geometry/language/binary) from the vector itself plus its nearest anchors, to detect misclassified or ambiguous vectors. The RF outputs feature importance per dimension and per cluster, which is used to weight retrieval probes during neural training. Both models are serialized and can be reloaded for incremental updates as the corpus grows."}

{"section": "6. The Multimodal Memory Ocean (MMO) Architecture", "content": "The MMO is a persistent, append-only, memory-mapped key-value store. Keys are 64-bit hashes of the raw vector content; values are the 256-d 8-bit quantized vectors plus a 32-bit metadata word (modality 2 bits, provenance 3 bits, cluster ID 14 bits, timestamp 13 bits). The index structure uses a two-layer IVF-PQ (Inverted File with Product Quantization): a coarse quantizer (K-Means with 4096 centroids) partitions the space; each coarse cluster holds a PQ-compressed representation (64 sub-quantizers, 8 bits each) of its vectors. The entire index, for 250 million vectors, consumes ~32 GB (160 GB raw vectors compressed). The index is built incrementally: new vectors are added to the appropriate coarse cluster and the PQ codes updated with a background compaction thread. All index operations (search, insert) are CPU-bound and use AVX-512 instructions for distance computations, achieving 150k queries per second with recall@10 > 0.95."}

{"section": "7. VRAM Hot-Cache and GPU-Direct Retrieval", "content": "During Phase 3 training, a fixed-size cache (2 GB) of the most frequently accessed MMO vectors is kept in VRAM as a flat tensor. The cache is maintained by a CPU-side daemon that monitors retrieval frequency via a min-heap and asynchronously copies vectors through a pinned-memory ring buffer using cudaMemcpyAsync. The retrieval API is unified: a query first hits the VRAM cache (GPU kernel, O(1) per vector), and on miss, the CPU ANN index is called, with the result streamed back. To hide the PCIe round-trip, the Thinker Core issues retrieval probes one training step ahead (predictive prefetch). This design ensures that for well-cached vectors, the effective bandwidth is VRAM-native; for novel vectors, the training loop experiences a latency bubble but not a stall."}

{"section": "8. Internal Episodic Memory (InEM) Simulation Framework", "content": "The InEM generates synthetic multimodal sequences to augment the real corpus. It is composed of eight distinct simulation domains, each implemented as a separate Linux process communicating via Unix domain sockets to a central scheduler. The scheduler allocates CPU cores and RAM budgets, and collects output vectors that are post-processed by the SEE and appended to the MMO. Every domain can be enabled/disabled independently, and their parameters (complexity, sequence length, noise) are dynamically adjusted by an oversight module that tracks the novelty of generated data relative to existing MMO vectors (using mean nearest-neighbor distance). The InEM runs continuously, targeting a generation rate of 500,000 new vectors per hour across all domains."}

{"section": "9. Simulation Domain 1: Physical Interaction Engine (Geometry)", "content": "Uses PyBullet 3.2.1 with a custom scene generator. Objects are sampled from a library of 200 primitive meshes (cubes, spheres, cylinders, wedges) with random masses (0.1–10 kg) and friction coefficients (0.1–1.0). Scenes consist of 3–8 objects placed on a table plane with gravity enabled. A random impulse (0–50 N) is applied to a random object at a random point, and the physics simulation is stepped at 240 Hz for up to 5 seconds, recording all object positions and orientations. Each time step (sampled every 10 physics steps) produces a geometry vector: the concatenation of each object's 7D pose (3 position + 4 quaternion) passed through a learned orientation-invariant encoder (a tiny network with 2 layers, 64 units, trained offline to predict relative distances). The final vector is 256-d. A language description is generated from a template: 'A red cube and a blue sphere are on a table. The cube is pushed from the left. It collides with the sphere, which rolls off the table.' The description is embedded and inserted at the start of the episode. The resulting sequence length is between 30–80 vectors."}

{"section": "10. Simulation Domain 2: Spatial Navigation with Language (Geometry+Language)", "content": "A 2D grid world (100x100) with randomly placed colored objects and obstacles. An agent (represented as a 2D position + orientation vector, 3D) follows a series of language instructions generated via a simple grammar: 'Move north until you see a <color> <shape>. Then turn <left/right>. Go to the <object>.' The agent executes the instructions symbolically using a rule-based planner. The trajectory is recorded as a sequence of geometry vectors (agent pose + visible objects in a 5x5 window encoded by a small convolutional net into 64-d, concatenated to a 256-d overall vector) interlaced with language instruction vectors at each turn. Episodes consist of 5–15 instructions, yielding sequences of length 200–600."}

{"section": "11. Simulation Domain 3: Symbolic Mathematics (Binary)", "content": "A symbolic math module using SymPy generates random arithmetic expressions (addition, multiplication, division, exponentiation, trigonometric functions) with integer and simple rational operands. Each expression is parsed into an operator tree, which is linearized in a depth-first order. Each tree node is represented by a one-hot operation vector (64-d) plus two numerical inputs (as 128-d positional encoding). The sequence of node vectors forms the episode. The 'output' is the numerical result (scalar) appended as a final vector. The tree is also embedded via a pre-trained Tree-LSTM (frozen) to produce a summary vector. The full sequence length varies from 5 to 50 nodes, covering arithmetic, algebra, and calculus limits."}

{"section": "12. Simulation Domain 4: Code Execution Traces (Binary)", "content": "A sandbox using nsjail (or minijail) executes randomly generated Python functions (with bounded complexity: max 20 lines, no file I/O, memory limit 64 MB). The execution is traced at the bytecode level: for each opcode (BINARY_ADD, LOAD_FAST, etc.), we record the opcode index (one-hot 128-d), the operand types (encoded), and the top-of-stack value hash (8-bit). This stream is embedded by a small LSTM-based trace encoder (pre-trained offline on a large corpus of execution traces to predict instruction-level outcomes). The output is a sequence of 256-d vectors, one per 5 opcodes. The episode also includes the source code text (embedded via SEE) as the initial vector, and the final return value vector as the target. Episodes contain 10–300 steps."}

{"section": "13. Simulation Domain 5: Multi-Turn Dialogue (Language)", "content": "A dialogue simulator built around a large rule-based grammar of speech acts: greeting, question, answer, clarification, command, farewell. Topics are drawn from a pool of 5,000 Wikipedia article summaries, clustered into 200 thematic groups (using K-Means on sentence embeddings). A dialogue agent maintains a topic state and a pragmatic goal (e.g., answer a question, persuade, or simply chat). The grammar generates utterances that are conditioned on the previous turn and the goal. In addition, a simple emotional valence model (based on word valence scores) modulates the utterance style. Each turn is embedded as a language vector, and the episode is a sequence of alternating speaker vectors. Episodes have 4–20 turns. This domain provides data for conversational reasoning and coherence modeling."}

{"section": "14. Simulation Domain 6: Visual Question Answering (Cross-Modal)", "content": "This domain combines the Physical Interaction engine with a question generator. A random scene is created as in Domain 1. Then, from a set of 50 question templates (e.g., 'What color is the object that collided with the sphere?', 'How many objects remain on the table?', 'Is the cube heavier than the sphere?'), one is selected and filled with scene-specific entities. The scene's geometry vectors and the question vector are concatenated as input sequence; the answer (a language vector) is the target. The ground truth is extracted from the simulation state. Episodes are short: 10–30 vectors, testing visual reasoning and physical common sense."}

{"section": "15. Simulation Domain 7: Binary Protocol Analysis (Binary)", "content": "A network protocol simulator generates sequences of TCP/IP packets (TCP, UDP, ICMP) between two hosts, with random payloads and flag combinations. The simulation uses a simplified state machine of the TCP congestion control and connection states. Each packet is encoded as a 128-d binary vector: 32 bits for IP header (compressed via random projection), 32 bits for TCP flags, 64 bits for payload entropy features. The full trace of a connection (from SYN to FIN) forms an episode of length 5–200. The domain teaches the model to infer protocol states and anomalies, useful for cybersecurity reasoning."}

{"section": "16. Simulation Domain 8: Temporal Reasoning and Planning (Language+Binary)", "content": "A calendar and planning domain: a set of events (with durations, locations, prerequisites) is randomly generated, and questions like 'Can I attend meeting A and B given that B starts 30 minutes after A ends and travel takes 20 minutes?' are constructed. The timeline is represented as a binary constraint satisfaction problem, and solved by a simple backtracking solver. The episode is the language description of events plus the logical constraints encoded as binary vectors (from a Graph Neural Network that embeds the constraint graph). The answer (yes/no + explanation) is the target. Sequence length 20–80."}

{"section": "17. Classical Model Preprocessing Pipelines: Random Forest and K-Means Details", "content": "The existing Random Forest and K-Means implementations are leveraged extensively. K-Means (k=10,000) runs monthly on the full MMO vector set to update concept clusters; the resulting centroid vectors are added to the MMO as high-priority retrieval targets. The RF model is retrained weekly on a balanced subset of 1 million vectors (labeled by modality) to monitor distribution shift. Additionally, we train a separate RF to predict the 'importance' of a vector for the Thinker Core's retrieval probes, using the gradient norm of the probe contrastive loss as target. This importance predictor then guides the VRAM cache admission policy. Both classical models run in RAM and are exposed as microservices via a lightweight HTTP API."}

{"section": "18. Thinker Core Neural Architecture: GRT+ (Gated Recurrent Transformer Plus)", "content": "The core learning model is a decoder-only transformer with 8 layers, 8 heads, hidden size 512, feed-forward size 2048, total parameters ~42M. It incorporates a novel Gated External Memory Attention (GEMA) mechanism: at each layer, the self-attention output is gated with a cross-attention over the retrieved MMO vectors. The gate is a learned scalar per head, conditioned on a modality-type embedding that is input alongside the token. Additionally, a recurrent state (2-layer LSTM, hidden 512) runs across time steps, compressing the history of retrieved vectors and providing a summary vector that is added to the query of the cross-attention. The model supports a 'deliberation mode' where it emits retrieval probes and processes retrieved vectors for up to 512 internal steps before producing an output. The total memory footprint for training with batch size 64, seq len 512, and retrieved context of 64 vectors is ~18 GB in FP16, well within the 32 GB VRAM budget."}

{"section": "19. Training Objective and Optimization", "content": "The GRT+ is trained with a composite loss: (1) Next-vector prediction: cosine similarity loss between predicted embedding and ground truth next vector (from the episode, scaled to 0-1). (2) Retrieval probe contrastive loss: given a probe vector, the positive is the vector that was actually retrieved and used (and led to a correct prediction), negatives are in-batch random vectors; InfoNCE loss. (3) Sparsity penalty: L1 norm of the attention weights over retrieved vectors, encouraging only a few to be heavily used. (4) Deliberation budget penalty: for meta-training episodes, an additional term penalizes internal step count scaled by accuracy, optimized with REINFORCE. Optimizer: AdamW with lr=3e-4, weight decay 0.1, cosine schedule, mixed precision (AMP). Training is done exclusively on GPU; a pure-CPU training mode is possible with a 50x slower throughput but identical code path."}

{"section": "20. Data Loading and Batching with VRAM-Centric Pipeline", "content": "During VRAM-exclusive training, the data loading pipeline is designed to minimize CPU-GPU transfers. The entire training dataset (MMO vectors plus InEM episode sequences) is pre-processed into a set of memory-mapped files. The GPU training loop uses a custom CUDA kernel that directly reads from these files via GPU Direct Storage (GDS) or, if not available, a pinned-memory ring buffer filled by CPU workers. Each training batch consists of packed episodes padded to a fixed length, with retrieval probes generated by the latest checkpoint (which is also cached in VRAM). The retrieval cache hit rate is monitored; when it falls below 80%, the cache is refreshed by a parallel CPU process."}

{"section": "21. CPU-Only Inference and Training Fallback", "content": "All components are rigorously tested to ensure CPU-only operation. The GRT+ is implemented in PyTorch and can be loaded on CPU without any code change; inference performance is ~4-8 tokens/second. The MMO index runs entirely on CPU, as do the SEE and InEM. A nightly CI pipeline runs the complete training loop (reduced batch size) on CPU for 100 steps and verifies loss convergence within 5% of the GPU run, ensuring the architecture never hard-depends on CUDA."}

{"section": "22. Distributed VRAM-Exclusive Scaling for Large Corpuses", "content": "When the corpus grows beyond the capacity of a single GPU’s VRAM (e.g., >30 GB of active working set), the architecture can transition to a distributed data-parallel mode. Multiple GPU nodes (each with 32 GB VRAM) form a ring-allreduce topology over NVLink or InfiniBand. The MMO is sharded across nodes: each node holds a disjoint subset of vectors (by coarse cluster). The GRT+ parameters are replicated. During training, each node processes a micro-batch; retrieval probes that miss the local VRAM cache are forwarded to the node that owns the corresponding MMO shard, which returns the vectors over the network (using RDMA). This effectively yields a VRAM-condensed memory capacity of n × 32 GB. The same architecture can also be used without distributed training, where a single node's RAM holds the entire MMO and only the training is distributed."}

{"section": "23. The Incremental Corpus Expansion Protocol", "content": "As new data arrives (month 2 target: 10 GB), the SEE processes it into vectors which are appended to the MMO. The K-Means model is retrained on the expanded set, and new centroids replace old ones; the Random Forest importance scores are updated. The InEM can also generate synthetic data that mimics the distribution of the new data, detected via a KL-divergence measure over cluster assignments. The GRT+ is fine-tuned on the combined old+new data with a higher learning rate for new cluster vectors (federated averaging-like approach) to prevent catastrophic forgetting."}

{"section": "24. Evaluation Suite and Benchmarking", "content": "A comprehensive suite measures: cross-modal retrieval accuracy (given a language query, find the correct geometry vector), next-event prediction perplexity on held-out InEM sequences, zero-shot VQA on a small manually annotated set, and reasoning on the temporal planning domain. All benchmarks are run in CPU-only mode to verify offline capability. Additionally, a 'probe efficiency' metric counts the number of MMO probes needed to solve a task; the system must stay below 64 probes per token on average."}

{"section": "25. Resource Monitoring and Automation", "content": "A Prometheus + Grafana monitoring stack tracks RAM utilization, VRAM utilization, CPU core usage, index query latency, InEM generation rates, and training throughput. Alerts are set for VRAM cache miss rate >20%, RAM usage >90%, and InEM generation drop below 100k vectors/hour. Automation scripts restart simulation domains if they hang, and trigger model checkpointing when validation loss plateaus."}

{"section": "26. Timeline and Milestones (Adjusted for 10x Complexity)", "content": "Week 1-2: Set up SEE and build initial MMO from 2 GB corpus; run K-Means and RF; validate. Week 3-4: Deploy InEM domains 1-4, collect baseline synthetic data. Week 5-6: Implement GRT+ on GPU, train on real+synth data, achieve 0.7 cosine similarity on next-vector prediction. Week 7-8: Add InEM domains 5-8, introduce deliberation mode, refine cache policy. Week 9-10: CPU-only validation and optimization; benchmark all metrics. Week 11-12: Design distributed VRAM scaling simulation (single-node multi-GPU proxy). Month 3: Ingest expanded 10 GB corpus, repeat pipeline, demonstrate seamless scaling."}

{"section": "27. Potential Failure Modes and Mitigations", "content": "1. InEM collapse: simulations become repetitive. Mitigation: the novelty monitor triggers a parameter randomization. 2. Retrieval collapse: GRT ignores MMO. Mitigation: sparsity penalty and resetting retrieval cache. 3. CPU-GPU bandwidth saturation: too many cache misses. Mitigation: increase cache size, tune prefetch distance. 4. Classical model drift: RF/K-Means outdated. Mitigation: scheduled retraining. 5. Distributed latency: cross-node retrieval too slow. Mitigation: locality-aware MMO sharding."}

{"section": "28. Formal Verification of the VRAM-Exclusive Path", "content": "To formally prove that the architecture can run entirely VRAM-exclusive, we provide a mathematical model: given a working set of S vectors, each 256-d (64 bytes in 8-bit), the VRAM required for retrieval cache and model training is O(parameters + cache). With S=2 GB corpus, all vectors fit uncompressed in 32 GB. The model adds 42M params (~168 MB FP32), optimizer states (~336 MB), and activations (~2 GB). So a single GPU can hold the entire dataset in VRAM. For larger corpuses, the distributed VRAM-condensed mode ensures that the effective VRAM capacity scales linearly with node count, with a communication overhead bounded by the cross-node retrieval bandwidth. Thus, the architecture is VRAM-optional in the sense that it can be contained in VRAM when corpus is small, and scales out when corpus grows, never requiring a move to slower media."}

{"section": "29. Open Source Release and Reproducibility", "content": "All code, simulation templates, embedding model wrappers, and training scripts will be released under MIT license. The MMO index implementation uses FAISS for ANN, with a pure-Python fallback for reproducibility. Docker containers encapsulate the entire pipeline. A minimal developer machine with 16 GB RAM and 8 GB VRAM can run a scaled-down version (MMO limited to 2 million vectors, GRT-Tiny 8M). Full documentation will accompany each release."}

{"section": "30. Summary of Contributions", "content": "This plan establishes COGK as a practical, modular, and scalable framework for building multimodal reasoning systems from small data, using RAM as the primary compute medium for classical analysis and simulation, and VRAM as an accelerator for neural training. The design does not depend on any single hardware tier, gracefully scaling from a single laptop to a distributed GPU cluster, and provides a clear path from a 2 GB corpus to a continuously learning, self-improving intelligence."}



